WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.417
Enter one API boundary. The client calls a public API address. The gateway provides a shared routing boundary instead of requiring clients to know every internal service location.

2
00:00:13.417 --> 00:00:25.750
Apply coarse policy. Validate the request and supported identity credentials with maintained security libraries. Reject oversized or invalid traffic before forwarding expensive work.

3
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:36.833
Route by responsibility. Event requests go to the event service; order requests go to the order service. API versions and paths should have deliberate routing rules.

4
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:49.000
Authorize the record. The order service checks ownership and allowed actions using verified identity. Passing gateway authentication does not grant access to another visitor order.

5
00:00:49.000 --> 00:01:01.083
Bound aggregation. If one API request fans out, set deadlines and partial-failure behavior. A slow optional service should not hold every gateway worker indefinitely.

6
00:01:01.083 --> 00:01:14.000
Operate the entry tier. Trace requests through the boundary and test gateway capacity and redundancy. Centralizing policy is useful, but it also creates an important operational dependency.
